00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.520
“My father had me sent to the family of the police&nbsp;
commissioner Lemde in Anklam in order to train me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.720
... One day Mr. Lemde came home with the news that&nbsp;
recruitment had been ordered and that the king had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.000
issued a nationwide conscription. My decision to&nbsp;
participate in the war, when it came to driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.960
out the intruders, had been taken some time&nbsp;
before. So now, when the opportunity presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.160
itself, I began to act. I planned how I should&nbsp;
go about this, so that I could leave the house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.280
without causing any trouble and get to the people&nbsp;
being recruited, who were gathering in droves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.720
When Lemde’s wife happened to catch me while I was&nbsp;
making male clothes, I pretended that I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.840
give them to my younger brother, who was my size.&nbsp;
When I had prepared everything, I cut off my long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.520
hair, and left wearing my man clothes, leaving&nbsp;
all my other belongings behind. In the darkness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.960
of the night I went to Jasenitz near Stettin, a large village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.640
on the Oder, where the recruiters were. Here I&nbsp;
pretended to be a boy tailor, and was promptly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.160
accepted and sent to the reserve battalion of the&nbsp;
Kolberg Regiment in Wollin, where I was trained.”

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.600
“The recruits and volunteers were first sent to&nbsp;
Kolberg ... Because there was no time for any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.560
actual thorough inspection - if the recruit&nbsp;
verbally agreed to serve in the military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.760
there were no further questions and they were&nbsp;
given a coat, a little mounting stand, a rifle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.840
a pouch with 60 cartridges and a slip of paper&nbsp;
that read; ‘Report to Captain v. Schmidt in Wollin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.040
for training and bring this and that with you.’ …&nbsp;
Among the fresh recruits who arrived at the camp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.280
there was a very handsome young person who was&nbsp;
assigned to the 4th company, and who, despite his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.960
lively temperament on the dance floor, otherwise&nbsp;
behaved well and diligently learned his duties.”

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.160
So began the story of a Napoleonic Wars&nbsp;
Prussian soldier called August Lübeck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.560
also known by her real name,&nbsp;
Sophie Dorothea Friederike Krüger.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.840
However, before we look at Sophie, and other women&nbsp;
who chose to join the army in the Revolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.840
and Napoleonic wars while pretending to&nbsp;
be men, it would be prudent to first look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.360
at the general history of women warriors in&nbsp;
Europe. So we first turn to the middle ages.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.560
The early middle ages were a very tumultuous&nbsp;
time. For a myriad of internal and external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.520
reasons the Western Roman Empire fell to&nbsp;
invading barbarians of mostly Germanic origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.240
and new political, social, and economic&nbsp;
institutions were formed. To what extent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.600
these ‘new’ institutions were connected to&nbsp;
classical, mostly Roman, institutions is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.880
debated and if you want to know more on that watch&nbsp;
the beginning of my Early Medieval Guilds video.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.120
The very patriarchal classical Roman&nbsp;
and Greek societies, with their quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:51.360
unquote 'professional’ armies, would never let&nbsp;
a woman fight, let alone lead an army to battle.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.280
But, in medieval Europe, with&nbsp;
the new institutions in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.560
this wasn’t necessarily the case. Even though&nbsp;
written evidence is hard to come by for early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.080
medieval Europe, some snippets, combined with&nbsp;
archeological records of warrior woman burials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.200
show that warrior women, who fought alongside men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:15.360
did exist. Most notably in Norse and Rus&nbsp;
societies but also in Germanic and other ones.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.360
One medieval monk wrote about&nbsp;
these Women Norse warriors stating&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.520
“There were once women in Denmark who&nbsp;
dressed themselves to look like men and&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.200
spent almost every minute cultivating soldiers'&nbsp;
skills; they did not want the sinews of&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.920
their valor to lose tautness and be infected&nbsp;
by self-indulgence. Loathing a dainty style&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.080
of living, they would harden body&nbsp;
and mind with toil and endurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.080
rejecting the
fickle pliancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.440
of girls and compelling their womanish&nbsp;
spirits to act with a virile ruthlessness.&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.400
They courted military fame so earnestly&nbsp;
that you would have guessed they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:57.840
unsexed themselves. Those especially&nbsp;
who had forceful personalities or were&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.960
tall and elegant, embarked on this way of&nbsp;
life. As if they were forgetful of their true&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.880
selves they put toughness before allure,&nbsp;
aimed at conflicts instead of kisses, tasted&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.320
blood, not lips, sought the clash of arms&nbsp;
rather than the arm's embrace, fitted to&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.200
weapons hands which should have been&nbsp;
weaving, desired not the couch but the kill,&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.360
and those they could have appeased&nbsp;
with looks they attacked with lances.”

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.840
Once most of Europe was christianized in the&nbsp;
high to late middle ages and the feudal system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:35.440
of favors, vassalages, and personal retenues&nbsp;
became the most common way of raising armies. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.520
warrior women did not disappear. In fact, thanks&nbsp;
to this more personalized system of army building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.080
warrior women seem to have become more common.&nbsp;
“The basic military unit throughout most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.200
Europe was the small group of warriors tied&nbsp;
to a lord by bonds of personal loyalty or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.200
vassalage — which often reinforced the still&nbsp;
deeper bonds of kinship”, therefore, warrior women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:03.040
could have been more common because the army was&nbsp;
essentially more domestic in character. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:09.120
no national conscription. If a lord needed people&nbsp;
to fight, and his household included women that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.960
were willing to take up arms for him, it was all&nbsp;
too easy and beneficial for him to agree to that.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.320
In the middle ages the majority of battles did&nbsp;
not happen on grand battlefields between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.600
massive armies. Most conflicts of the middle&nbsp;
ages were small battles and skirmishes between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.480
various feuding lords, towns, and cities,&nbsp;
occurring among the farmlands of Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.760
A couple dozen extra people in your army could&nbsp;
be the difference between victory and defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.080
and, in that case, it did not matter much&nbsp;
whether these couple dozen extra people were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:46.640
women warrior known as bellatrices. The plural&nbsp;
of bellatrix, a latin word meaning warrior women.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.000
This is why, as stated by Frances and Joseph&nbsp;
Gies, “the participation of armed ladies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.040
[in war]…was considered, when everything&nbsp;
is taken into account, as fairly normal.”

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.200
In fact, not only was the participation&nbsp;
of women in combat considered normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.840
but women leading a medieval army was&nbsp;
also fairly normal. The most famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.240
example everyone knows about is Joan&nbsp;
of Arc but there were plenty of others.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.520
Aethelflaed, the daughter of Alfred the Great,&nbsp;
led an army with her brother Edward attacking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.720
Scandinavians settled in the north of England.
“A Lombard princess Sichelgaita was said to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.520
present a ‘fearsome’ sight when dressed in&nbsp;
full armor, and was credited with rallying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.800
her husband's men at the siege of Durazzo&nbsp;
in 1081 by chasing after the enemies on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.000
horseback and threatening them with her spear.”
There was also Therasia of Portugal who raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
and led her own army purely because&nbsp;
she just wanted to conquer more land.&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.080
And Matilda of Tuscany, led an army&nbsp;
in defence of the pope and was said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:49.040
to have been the "most prudent war-leader&nbsp;
and most faithful soldier of St. Peter."

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.120
There was even an early 15th century&nbsp;
handbook written by a noble women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.440
for other noble women which stated that “she, [a&nbsp;
noble woman], ought to have the heart of a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.080
that is, she ought to know how to use weapons and&nbsp;
be familiar with everything that pertains to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.440
so that she may be ready to&nbsp;
command her men if the need arises.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.880
She should know how to launch an&nbsp;
attack or to defend against one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.160
if the situation calls for it. She should take&nbsp;
care that her fortresses are well garrisoned.”

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.240
With all of this said, however, with the&nbsp;
fact that warrior women or women leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.880
armies wouldn't be strange in medieval&nbsp;
Europe, there’s much more nuance to this.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:28.480
The occupation of a warrior was always seen as&nbsp;
a male job. Even on occasions when gender roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:35.360
had been loosen, like in the middle ages, women&nbsp;
warriors, even though not unusual for any soldier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.880
to see on the battlefield, weren’t numerous in&nbsp;
any stretch of the imagination. Bellatrices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.200
IF present in a battle, always&nbsp;
constituted a small minority of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.480
army. A minority whose presents wouldn't&nbsp;
be unusual, but a minority nonetheless.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:56.000
On top of that, there did always exist writings&nbsp;
and people that would chastise women who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.960
actively partook in battle. Accusing them of being&nbsp;
‘“forgetful of their true selves," in that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.240
"cultivated soldiers' skills"’. One anonymous&nbsp;
account of the third crusade stated that woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.120
couldn’t and shouldn’t be warriors in the&nbsp;
crusades “because of the fragility of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.400
sex.” Other accounts accused warrior women&nbsp;
of sexual misconduct or even witchcraft.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.800
This is why, by the time of the early modern&nbsp;
period, when “the small group of warriors tied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.520
to a lord by bonds of personal loyalty or&nbsp;
vassalage” became to be replaced with more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.480
professionalized armies and mercenaries, and&nbsp;
the renaissance began to reinforce the gender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.920
roles of the classical period more thoroughly,&nbsp;
woman warriors started to be no longer accepted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.160
And so, starting in the 16th century,&nbsp;
women warriors not only disappeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:47.120
as active participants of armies but&nbsp;
also faded into historical obscurity.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.320
The role of women, in armies, in the early modern&nbsp;
period, became to be almost solely supportive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.840
Women were part of the baggage train, known as&nbsp;
‘camp followers’, that followed the army around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.800
They provided nursing and production support,&nbsp;
“logistical support and sexual favours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.280
and acted as entrenchment workers.” Women&nbsp;
already did this in the middle ages but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.640
by the time of the early modern period, it became&nbsp;
the only thing they did. A woman’s role in a war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.920
increasingly came to be viewed through a quote&nbsp;
‘natural gender order’. That is, just as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.360
perceived domestic role of women was to be the&nbsp;
support of the head of the household, the man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.720
so were the women in armies supposed to be&nbsp;
only a support to the soldiers, the men.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.040
But even this supportive role of women in&nbsp;
armies started to change by the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.320
early modern period. Women's involvement with&nbsp;
any kind of military action started to be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.600
as a nuisance that only distracted the rest of&nbsp;
the men. And as militaries became increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.040
professionalized, standardized, and modernized,&nbsp;
women involvement with the military decreased.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.040
I am glossing over a lot of history&nbsp;
here, especially on how gender systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.200
evolved and changed with things like the&nbsp;
protestant reformation and the enlightenment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:01.040
all of which, of course, also affected&nbsp;
women's position within the military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:04.480
but there’s just no time to get into&nbsp;
that. So just be aware of it happening.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.920
By the time of the late early modern period,&nbsp;
the only way a woman could see military action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.840
was by crossdressing and posing as a man.&nbsp;
Which did happen but very rarely. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.200
only one instance of it happening is known in&nbsp;
today’s Germany and only 16 instances in Britain.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.280
However, at the end of the 18th century&nbsp;
and the start of the 19th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:30.880
there began an active push for women involvement&nbsp;
in war, correlated with an increase of women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.520
crossdressing to get into active combat.&nbsp;
Which brings us back to Sophie Krüger.

