00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:59.520
So why did so many women, like&nbsp;
the aforementioned Sophie Krüger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.800
suddenly decide they wanted to fight? Well,&nbsp;
the answer to that is very multifaceted.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:10.000
As stated by Hagemann, women's decisions to&nbsp;
join the army could have been multiple. “Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.840
women were driven by social and economic hardship.&nbsp;
Others took up arms to defend their own property.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.560
Some sought adventure, others to escape&nbsp;
the narrow confines of female existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:26.160
and some followed lovers or husbands into&nbsp;
the fray”, etc. However, all these reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:32.160
for women wanting to fight, existed even before&nbsp;
the modern period, so why the change now? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:36.640
what the Revolutionary and Napoleonic&nbsp;
wars provided, that wasn’t there before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:43.840
was a restructuring or, at least, questioning of&nbsp;
core social dynamics and ideas all across Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:47.840
Ever since the French revolution,&nbsp;
the status quo was being challenged.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:53.600
The superficial individual loyalties to a monarch,&nbsp;
to a people’s sovereign, started to be superseded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:59.920
by one's loyalty to a perceived ethnic kinship,&nbsp;
to one’s own “peoples”, to a nation. A nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:06.480
which theoretically, was composed of equals, of&nbsp;
both women and men. This meant that the period of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:12.080
the Revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars provided&nbsp;
a nationalistic drive, for mostly young women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:18.320
to fight for a perceived single and equal national&nbsp;
identity. This was correlated with the push by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:23.600
women towards equal rights not only in politics&nbsp;
or the workplace but also in the military.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:30.000
For example, it was the first time in history that&nbsp;
a quote “Army of Amazons” was seriously tabled to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.280
national governments by women in countries like&nbsp;
France and Prussia. It was also the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:40.720
for most European countries that a nationwide&nbsp;
conscription was enacted, the administration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:46.560
which was poor in favour of quick recruitment and&nbsp;
so many women could easily enlist without being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:51.840
found out. These conscriptions were clearly&nbsp;
meant for men but some nationalistic women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:57.120
like Sophie Kruger, felt like they had the&nbsp;
patriotic right to sign up, and so they did.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:02.800
The already existent reasons for women wanting&nbsp;
to fight, combined with the rise of nationalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:08.560
across Europe and the enacting of first national&nbsp;
conscriptions, created a situation which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:13.600
ripe for an increase in women crossdressing&nbsp;
and joining the army. It is prudent to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.480
however, that even though there was an increase in&nbsp;
women crossdressing to join the army at this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.040
it was still a very marginal number and&nbsp;
most women involvement with the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.680
and the military, was on the home&nbsp;
front (or as camp followers).

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:30.000
Nonetheless, it was in this context, when&nbsp;
the social order was being questioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.200
in Europe, that Sophie Krüger, or August Lübeck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:39.280
enlisted, so that she could help quote “drive out&nbsp;
the intruders”, who in her case were the French.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.720
August Lübeck was found to be a girl even before&nbsp;
the training of the regiment ended. Both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:50.560
captain of the regiment v. Schmidt and the general&nbsp;
v. Borstell confronted Sophie Krüger and ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:55.920
her to leave the army. To which Sophie replied&nbsp;
“If I am not allowed to remain a soldier here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:01.760
I will be accepted elsewhere”. Seeing that there&nbsp;
was no convincing Sophie. The officers reluctantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:06.960
told her if she continued to dress and act as&nbsp;
August Lübeck she could remain with the company.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:07.120
But you must still act and dress as August Lübeck.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.920
August Lübeck participated&nbsp;
in multiple battles of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:14.640
Napoleonic wars and showed particular&nbsp;
bravery at the Battle of Großbeeren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.480
and later the Battle of Dennewitz, the&nbsp;
story of which I will recount here.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.920
During the Battle of Dennewitz, the Kolberg&nbsp;
regiment came under heavy fire from an enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:31.200
force. They hid in a mill in the village of&nbsp;
Boelsdorf, severely weakened. The commanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.160
officer saw that the only chance the regiment&nbsp;
had of winning was to seize the enemy cannons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:41.520
and so asked for volunteers for this attack.&nbsp;
“August Lübeck was the first to come forward;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.960
seven other men from the much weakened company&nbsp;
joined her, and after that several more followed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:52.240
After a short advance towards the cannons a piece&nbsp;
of the rifle of the man next to Lubeck shattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:58.400
from an enemy bullet and the shrapnel violently&nbsp;
hit Lubeck in her left eye. Soon afterwards she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:04.720
was wounded to the bone in the left shoulder by&nbsp;
a blow from a grenade, and her arm was paralyzed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:10.000
Wrapping her wound with a handkerchief and&nbsp;
taking the rifle under solely her right arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:14.880
she rejoined her battalion, which was&nbsp;
now being repulsed and tried to regroupe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:20.000
Half an hour later [after the battalion regrouped]&nbsp;
they advanced again towards the cannons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:25.200
she was hit by a blow in the right foot, the&nbsp;
force of which turned her around in a circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:30.480
and knocked her down on the ground. However,&nbsp;
even before a medic, who rushed to her aid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:35.920
had time to stop the bleeding and bandage her&nbsp;
up properly she pulled herself up again and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:40.640
joined in the pursuit of the enemy with her&nbsp;
regiment which … had secured the cannons”.&nbsp;

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:47.040
After she recovered from her injuries, she fought&nbsp;
in the 1814 campaigns, entered Paris as part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:52.640
the Coalition troops, and, for her enormous&nbsp;
bravery, received the Iron Cross Second Class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:58.320
and the Russian Order of St. George. Then, a&nbsp;
year later, she fought in the Waterloo Campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:03.040
helping to defeat Napoleon for good.&nbsp;
After that she retired from the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:08.000
whereupon her story became public and she&nbsp;
became an overnight sensation in Prussia.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:15.200
But August Lübeck, or Sophie Krüger,&nbsp;
wasn’t the only woman we know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:17.680
that fought in the Prussian army during this time.

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:23.120
There was Anna Lühring who, under the name&nbsp;
Eduard Kruse, fought for the Lützow Free Corps.&nbsp;

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:26.880
There was Maria Werder who crossdressed&nbsp;
and followed her husband to war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:30.400
and attained the highest rank possible&nbsp;
for a non-commissioned officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:34.320
she was a sergeant major in the&nbsp;
Second Silesian Hussar Regiment.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:40.240
And most notably there was Eleonore Prochaska&nbsp;
who, while serving as an infantryman, died at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:45.600
the battle of Göhrde. Once it was revealed that&nbsp;
she was a woman, and that she died in a battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:50.949
fighting for her nation, many patriotic poems and&nbsp;
stories were written about her across the German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.949 --> 00:17:57.040
speaking lands. This Heldenjungfrau ‘Heroic&nbsp;
Virgin’ or ‘Heroic Maiden’, became so famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:02.720
that Ludwig van Beethoven even composed&nbsp;
music to a play about her romentesized life.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:07.520
These warrior women were also not unique&nbsp;
in the Prussian and greater German armies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:11.760
There were around 120 known instances&nbsp;
of crossdressing French women in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:16.960
Revolutionary and Napoleonic Armies. There was&nbsp;
also Franziska Scanagatta who was Italian and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:21.040
served in the Austrian army, there was Marie&nbsp;
Schellinck who was from today’s Belgium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:26.000
there was Nadezhda Durova from today’s&nbsp;
Ukraine who served in the Russian army, etc.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:31.600
There were, of course, also many more women that&nbsp;
we just don’t know about, because, after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:37.200
they were trying to hide. By the pure nature&nbsp;
of this topic we will never know how many women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:42.960
actually enlisted in those first drafts of Europe&nbsp;
and how many of them fought acting as men purely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:47.200
because they were told by the establishment that&nbsp;
they couldn’t fight for their “nation” as women.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:51.840
Those aforementioned proposals for the “Army&nbsp;
of Amazons” during the Revolutionary and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:57.280
Napoleonic era were always quickly shot down by&nbsp;
all the European governments. With one Parisian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:02.800
newspaper Les Revolutions de Paris writing&nbsp;
that “A woman belongs at home with her family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:08.240
or household . . . weapons must be forbidden&nbsp;
to women.” Such allegations were attacked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:12.720
one Parisian woman, Pauline Leon, who, in&nbsp;
front of the French Legislative Assembly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:18.400
stated that “How can we be denied the sweetness of&nbsp;
avenging our fathers, husbands, and brothers or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.680
of dying at their sides? The Declaration of the&nbsp;
Rights of Man give us this right, unless these do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:29.280
not concern women?” She was given an applause&nbsp;
from the Assembly but her request was denied.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:35.040
Despite nationalistic zeal sweeping across Europe,&nbsp;
despite the status que being questioned or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:40.560
in some places, actively changed, the classical&nbsp;
gender roles still remained deeply entranched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:46.320
in European society. On top of that, the rise of&nbsp;
nationalism, that spurred on some women to act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:53.120
also changed, or evolved, a perceived defining&nbsp;
factor of masculinity pertaining to a man’s duty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:59.120
as the protector. No longer was a man viewed as&nbsp;
just the protector of his family or his household,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:04.960
his protective duties expanded to encompass&nbsp;
the greater “ethnic family”, the nation.&nbsp;

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:10.320
So in that sense, the warrior women were&nbsp;
seen as actively undermining the male role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:15.680
as protectors of the nation. “To display&nbsp;
female strength openly appeared to invoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:20.640
the complementary notion of male weakness –&nbsp;
a provocation that was bound to seem all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:25.040
more aggressive since the call to arms&nbsp;
for collective self-defence of female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:29.600
honour suggested that male protectors&nbsp;
were either inadequate or superfluous.”

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:34.080
On top of warrior women challenging the&nbsp;
male notion as the protectors of the nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:38.800
female want for weapons and active&nbsp;
participation in war was one more step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:42.160
towards equal rights with men. As Hangmann states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:47.920
“The French Revolution had shown already how&nbsp;
short the leap was from calls for arms for women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:53.760
to demands for civil equality with men.” A thing&nbsp;
that the men in charge were seldom eager to do.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.240
The few (relatively speaking) women heroines&nbsp;
like Eleonore Prochaska or Sophie Krüger, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:04.480
were praised for their bravery and became national&nbsp;
heroes, but not without opponents, were very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:11.120
so treated as the exceptions that proved the rule&nbsp;
of established gender roles. Therefore, even these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:17.280
Heldenjungfrauen were always reminded of their&nbsp;
place in society. For example, when Sophie Krüger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:23.120
retired and shortly after got married, Fredrik&nbsp;
William the 3rd of Prussia payed for her dowry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:28.560
while sending her a letter which had the lines&nbsp;
‘‘May she now forget the non-commissioned office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:34.960
obey her husband and always keep in mind the words&nbsp;
of the Holy Scripture: ‘He shall rule over you!’”

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:39.280
These heroic warrior women, who were&nbsp;
seen as national heroes, challenged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:44.720
the established gender order in Europe and as&nbsp;
such, they, along with the population at large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:49.440
had to be reminded of their quote&nbsp;
“true place” in the gender hierarchy.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:56.400
Of course, this entire topic is very nuanced,&nbsp;
and as McLaughlin states “gender systems affect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.400 --> 00:22:01.200
everyone who comes into contact with them —&nbsp;
those who conform to expected roles as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:06.400
as those who engage in anomalous behavior” and&nbsp;
in that sense there where also plenty of women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:11.200
who agreed with the status quo, and even&nbsp;
condemned the women who wanted to fight.

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:17.200
One woman stated in a newspaper in 1813 that&nbsp;
“No, Sisters! Let us instead take on tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:22.240
more appropriate to us, let us care for those&nbsp;
injured in war, for there we can do much good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:27.120
Women seem to have been created to alleviate&nbsp;
man’s pain through patient attendance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:31.760
gentle care [. . .] Femininity is a woman's&nbsp;
greatest charm. It would be immediately lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:36.960
in the tumult of battle and camp, and the happy&nbsp;
future for which our men and boys are fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:42.160
would perhaps no longer include the accustomed&nbsp;
comforts of female company. For that reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:44.000
[let there be] no
army of Amazons!”

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:51.760
With all that said, however, the most ironic part&nbsp;
of this, perhaps, is that the “Male officers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:56.720
administrators [who fought alongside women]&nbsp;
were often surprisingly ambivalent towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:01.680
such cross-dressing … and tolerated their&nbsp;
deception even after it had been discovered.”

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:06.800
I will end with a quote from the&nbsp;
commanding officer of Sophie Kruger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:11.280
Captain v. Schmidt, “One day an&nbsp;
officer approached me and said:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:16.000
‘Your compatriot: Kruger, is one of the most&nbsp;
well-behaved non-commissioned officers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:20.960
regiment and is the only one in the regiment to&nbsp;
have earned the Iron Cross. Whenever there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:26.560
a call for volunteers, for some daring act,&nbsp;
she was always the first to step forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:32.000
and her bold courage and presence of mind&nbsp;
often aroused admiration. She also commands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:38.320
great respect from the whole regiment for her&nbsp;
impeccable, morally pure, performance. Truly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:43.600
she lives up to her hometown, Friedland&nbsp;
should be proud to have such a daughter.’”

00:23:50.465 --> 00:23:53.345
long time ago for reading some&nbsp;
of the quotes in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

